# Line endings.
#
# This file exists because its absence broke a production deploy on
# 2026-10-22. Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so a clone on
# Windows checked the Dockerfile out with CRLF. The Dockerfile WRITES the
# Fly release command with a heredoc:
#
#     RUN cat > /usr/local/bin/release-migrate <<'SH'
#     #!/bin/sh
#     ...
#
# so the script inherited the Dockerfile's line endings and its shebang
# became "#!/bin/sh\r". Linux then looked for an interpreter literally
# named "/bin/sh\r", and the deploy died with:
#
#     [dumb-init] /usr/local/bin/release-migrate: No such file or directory
#
# which names a file that is right there, with 0755 on it. Nothing in the
# message points at line endings, and the same commit deploys cleanly from
# Linux, so this is only reproducible on a Windows checkout.
#
# Everything in this repository is built and run inside a Linux container,
# so the default is LF on checkout everywhere, not just in the index.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Except the scripts Windows itself runs. PowerShell handles LF fine, but
# CRLF is what the platform's own tooling writes, and nothing ships these
# into an image.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never touched. Normalising a binary corrupts it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
